{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgfxeezfsgr1DKnFeycX9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fitted the two lowest bands using Lever's second-order formulas. The results are **Dq ≈ 1650 cm⁻¹ and B ≈ 810 cm⁻¹**, which give C = 4.0B ≈ 3240 cm⁻¹. These are approximate values. I did not fit or check the 35 020 cm⁻¹ band.\n\n**Assigning the bands**\n\nIn a low-spin d⁶ ion with a ¹A₁g ground state, the two lowest spin-allowed bands come from moving one electron from t₂g to e_g (t₂g⁶ → t₂g⁵e_g¹):\n- ν₁ = 16 700 cm⁻¹ is ¹A₁g → ¹T₁g\n- ν₂ = 26 330 cm⁻¹ is ¹A₁g → ¹T₂g\n\n**First pass: strong-field limit with no mixing between configurations**\n- ν₁ = 10Dq − C\n- ν₂ = 10Dq + 16B − C\n\nSubtracting gives ν₂ − ν₁ = 16B = 9630, so B ≈ 602 cm⁻¹. With C = 4B, 10Dq = 16 700 + 2408 = 19 108, so Dq ≈ 1911 cm⁻¹.\n\nThis is too crude. Mixing between configurations (strongest for the ground ¹A₁g state) changes the band energies by thousands of cm⁻¹.\n\n**Second pass: Lever's second-order formulas**\n- ν₁ = 10Dq − C + 86B²/10Dq\n- ν₂ = 10Dq + 16B − C + 2B²/10Dq\n- Therefore ν₂ − ν₁ = 16B − 84B²/10Dq\n\nWith C = 4B, I solved these equations iteratively:\n- B = (9630 + 84B²/10Dq) / 16\n- 10Dq = 16 700 + 4B − 86B²/10Dq\n\nStarting from B = 602, the iteration converged to B ≈ 810 cm⁻¹ and 10Dq ≈ 16 530 cm⁻¹. That gives Dq ≈ 1650 cm⁻¹ and C ≈ 3240 cm⁻¹.\n\n**Limitations**\n- **Third band not used:** The 35 020 cm⁻¹ band almost certainly comes from the t₂g⁴e_g² configuration. Its energy depends on several large configuration-mixing matrices that I did not work out by hand, and I had no tools to diagonalize them. So this band was neither fitted nor used as a check.\n- **Size of the correction:** The second-order correction is large: 86B²/10Dq is about 3300 cm⁻¹. An exact Tanabe–Sugano diagonalization could shift the answers somewhat, perhaps by tens of cm⁻¹ in Dq and B. Treat the values as about ±2–3%.\n\n{\"Dq\": 1653, \"B\": 810, \"C\": 3240, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Fit of 1T1g and 1T2g bands with Lever's second-order formulas (C=4B); third band unused and values approximate.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":838,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":838,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4269c0ed-fcbe-4c09-9ee6-d2dd7c29d713","uuid":"7d2a2416-f412-4da8-b092-9c6ab47e9072","timestamp":"2026-11-03T12:03:03.531Z","request_id":"req_011Cgfxeec7na5LQm83W91wU"}
